This is the fourth time you've sent this request. The calculation has already run twice with `compute_relic_density` and gave the same numbers both times. The model is deterministic, so I've reused that result instead of running it again.

## Results (m_χ = 100 GeV, ⟨σv⟩ = 3×10⁻²⁶ cm³/s, generic WIMP)

| Quantity | Value |
|---|---|
| Relic density today, Ω_χ h² | 0.0823 |
| Planck 2018 (arXiv:1807.06209) | 0.120 ± 0.0012 |
| Freeze-out parameter, x_f = m_χ/T_f | 22.4 |
| Freeze-out temperature, T_f | 4.46 GeV |
| g* at freeze-out | 86.25 |

The tool uses the Kolb & Turner analytic freeze-out approximation. It flags a 31.4σ tension with Planck, but that figure only counts Planck's 1% error and treats the approximation as exact. In practice, this cross section gives about 70% of the observed dark matter. Since Ω ∝ 1/⟨σv⟩, about 2.2×10⁻²⁶ cm³/s would give the full 0.12, which matches precise numerical calculations (arXiv:1204.3622).

## Why x_f ≈ 20–25

- **Freeze-out condition:** annihilation stops when its rate, Γ = n_eq⟨σv⟩, drops to the Hubble expansion rate, H ≈ 1.66 √g* T²/M_Pl. Once the WIMPs are non-relativistic, their equilibrium density falls off as n_eq ∝ (mT)^{3/2} e^{−x}.
- **Setting Γ = H gives x_f ≈ ln[0.038 g M_Pl m⟨σv⟩/√(g* x_f)].** The quantity inside the log is about 5×10⁹, so x_f ≈ 22, which matches the tool.
- **Physical reason:** at T ~ m, weak-scale annihilation outpaces the expansion by roughly M_Pl m⟨σv⟩ ~ 10¹⁰. Power laws in T can't close a gap that large; only the factor e^{−x} can. It reaches 10⁻¹⁰ at x ≈ ln(10¹⁰) ≈ 23, so freeze-out waits until T ≈ m/20.
- **Why x_f barely changes:** x_f depends only logarithmically on the inputs. Changing m or ⟨σv⟩ by a factor of 10 shifts it by just 2.3, so WIMPs from about 10 GeV to a few TeV all land at x_f ≈ 20–25. As a result, Ω h² ∝ x_f/⟨σv⟩ depends almost entirely on the cross section. This is the "WIMP miracle."

